{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Use IAM access advisor with AWS Organizations to set permission guardrails confidently","context":"The document explains the use of AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) Access Advisor in conjunction with AWS Organizations to establish permission guardrails. IAM Access Advisor employs data analysis to help organizations determine infrequently used services and set restrictions using service control policies (SCPs). The service last accessed information for AWS Organizations can be reviewed in the IAM console or programmatically using IAM Access Advisor APIs","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2019/06/now-use-iam-access-advisor-with-aws-organizations-to-set-permission-guardrails-confidently/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"AWS Security Hub now uncovers identity risks from unused access - AWS","context":"AWS Security Hub has expanded its capabilities to include the detection of identity risks related to unused IAM permissions, roles, and credentials across an AWS organization. This new feature enables central security teams to manage identity risks alongside threats, exposures, and posture findings in a unified console. Previously, managing identity risk at scale required toggling between multiple tools without a unified view. With Security Hub, identity risks are now surfaced alongside other findings, allowing teams to prioritize remediation based on actual organizational risk. When Security Hub is enabled for an organization, a service-linked IAM Access Analyzer is created in each member account with no additional configuration required. Security Hub evaluates IAM principals against 90 days of actual access activity, detects unused access, and correlates identity findings with exposure context. Additionally, Security Hub provides on-demand generation of recommended least-privilege policies based on actual usage patterns to help teams refine IAM permissions and reduce their attack surface. These capabilities represent a foundational step towards broader cloud infrastructure entitlement management in Security Hub, delivered with consistent workflows, automation rules, and downstream integrations. These features are included with Security Hub Essentials at no additional cost. For more information, see the \"Understanding unused access findings in Security Hub\" section in the AWS Security Hub User Guide and the AWS Security Hub product page. Security Hub is available in various AWS regions as listed on the AWS Regional Services List","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/aws-security-hub-unused-access/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region","skill_description":"Enables a multi-region AWS CloudTrail trail with S3 log storage, CloudWatch Logs integration, and CloudWatch Logs Insights queries for security monitoring and compliance auditing. Use when setting up centralized API activity logging across all AWS regions.\n\nServices: cloudtrail, s3, cloudwatch, iam, kms\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: security, monitoring\n*","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudtrail-multi-region"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"aws-investigation","skill_description":"Investigates what is happening in an AWS account from evidence already recorded there. Not for capturing new data from running code (snapshots of function arguments on a live service, Dynamic Instrumentation), questions answered from the CloudWatch Omni context graph or Application Signals service map, or building or changing infrastructure. Covers reading deployment records instead of the newest stored version (completed AppConfig deployments, the version an alias points to, a stack's current template), following a request through every service's own logs (exact per-layer fields, unique failed requests in an alarm's breach window, failure boundary per event), and answering questions from the account's data (exact counts and breakdowns, coverage of the requested range, exact filters and joins, the rule behind failed or unmatched records, verbatim values). Use to triage an alarm, error spike or failed request, trace a request across services, or count, break down, extract or join records.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, appconfig, lambda, cloudformation, dynamodb, s3, athena, glue\nTasks: debug, investigate, query, troubleshoot\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability, operations","skill_name":"aws-investigation"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"querying-aws-cloudwatch","skill_description":"Runs SQL queries on CloudWatch Logs data exported as Apache Iceberg tables in S3 Tables. Covers VPC Flow Logs, WAF logs, CloudFront access logs, Route 53 resolver logs, Network Firewall logs, EKS audit logs, Verified Access logs, SES logs, VPC Lattice logs, Step Functions logs, NLB access logs, S3 Server Access Logs, and 20+ other AWS vended data sources. Applies when analyzing network traffic, investigating security incidents, querying exported logs with SQL, enabling S3 Tables integration, configuring log export, correlating logs with other data, running Athena queries on the aws-cloudwatch table bucket, analyzing S3 access patterns, S3 latency investigation, S3 cost attribution, or bucket access auditing.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, s3tables, athena, glue, s3\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, audit, configure\nPersona: developer, data-engineer, devops\nWorkload: data-analytics, observability\n*","skill_name":"querying-aws-cloudwatch"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify IAM Access Advisor is the right tool to check role usage"}
